The control system around an AI agent
CONTEXT • TOOLS • STATE • MEMORY • CONSTRAINTS • OBSERVABILITY • VERIFICATION • EVALS

Harness engineering is the end-to-end design of the environment around an LLM agent: the context it sees, the tools it can use, the constraints that bound it, the state it preserves, and the feedback signals that verify and improve its work.

1. DEFINITION AND SCOPE
Harness engineering is the engineering discipline of shaping the environment, interfaces, constraints, tools, state, and feedback loops around an LLM so it can do useful work, detect mistakes, and improve through correction.

It goes beyond prompt engineering. Prompt engineering focuses on how to ask. Harness engineering also shapes what the model can see, what it can do, how its actions are checked, and how failures are turned into better future behavior.

IN SCOPE
context construction • tool use • execution environment • state and memory • guardrails and approvals • observability • evaluation

OUT OF SCOPE
model training and architecture internals, except where they affect use

2. CLOSED-LOOP CONTROL VIEW
Intent / spec → Guide the agent → Agent acts in environment → Evidence returns → Verify / review / approve → Update state / memory → Act again or stop

Closed-loop execution and correction

FEEDFORWARD (BEFORE ACTION)
• clear task specification and success criteria
• relevant context, examples, and constraints
• well-defined tools, schemas, and permissions
• policy boundaries and stop conditions

FEEDBACK (AFTER ACTION)
• verify outputs with tests, checks, or review
• inspect traces, tool calls, logs, and artifacts
• measure quality, safety, latency, and cost
• update state, memory, and future guidance

Feedforward controls reduce the probability of failure. Feedback controls detect outcomes and enable correction.

